  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Characters that are studied by biologists are of three types. Traits that are distributed into a range of discrete classes, such as scale counts in fish or leaf number in plants, are called meristic characters. Those that are measured on a continuous scale are known as metric characters. Length, weight, and growth rate attributes are examples of the latter. Attributes such as survival to a fixed age are known as all-or-none or binary characters. Of course, due to technical limitations, even measures of truly continuously distributed traits must always be artificially placed into discrete categories. Meter sticks, for example, are unable to distinguish between individuals that are 25.2 and 25.3 mm in length. Both would typically be placed in the 25–26 mm category, although the biological reality is that every conceivable length in the 25–26 mm range is possible.",
      "source_page": 36,
      "printed_page": 16,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter2:p001:b3",
        "Genetics_chapter2:p001:b4"
      ],
