          1411.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "5d86276b698ffeab8a4a9156fff5daa7174f51e5e722ad9e29ae27385e21400e"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "If, on the other hand, $z$ is a continuously distributed variable (as with maternal weight), $P(z = z_i)$ makes no sense since the probability that $z$ takes on any specific value is infinitesimally small. It is more meaningful to consider the probability that $z$ lies within a specific range of values, say $z_1$ and $z_2$. This quantity is described by the probability density function $ p(z) $, which satisfies the integral",
