      "content_sha256": "1a7d190b418fc4d503a5c2e7c08e9a47ff9577858eee253dbaaae96cf2658bae"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Quantitative geneticists generally rely on the variance as a measure of the dispersion of a distribution. However, additional moments can be informative. For example, the third moment about the mean ( $ \\mu_{3} $) is a useful measure of the asymmetry of a distribution. Also known as the skewness, $ \\mu_{3} $ is the expected cubic deviation from the mean. As in the case of the variance, it can be expressed in terms of the moments about the origin,",
