    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "is a general expression for the $r$th moment about the mean. It also follows that $\\mu_r$ can always be expressed in terms of moments about the origin [ $E(z)$, $E(z^2)$, $\\ldots$, $E(z^r)$]. As was shown for the variance and the skewness, these terms are obtainable from the binomial expansion of $(z - \\mu)^r$.",
